  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1133 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nThe latest RAP analysis and radar and satellite imagery show a \ncold front moving over Lower Michigan this afternoon, while \nsurface high pressure builds in from the central Plains. Behind \nthe front, cold advection and Lake Superior moisture fluxes are \nkeeping strato-cumulus clouds scattered to broken over north-\ncentral Wisconsin, though these clouds will mix out later today. \nHigh pressure over the central Plains will migrate into the Great \nLakes tonight, while a Pacific cold front will approach the region\nby Tuesday.\n\nFrost And Near-Freezing Temperatures Tonight:\nAs high pressure moves into the region late tonight, clear skies\nand precipitable water values at 50 to 60 percent of normal will\ncreate decent radiational cooling conditions. Winds remain the\nprimary uncertainty, as statistical guidance suggests a light 3 to\n4 knot breeze lingering overnight before decoupling toward\ndaybreak. This slight breeze may keep temperatures from dropping \nas low as blended model guidance indicates, keeping confidence too\nlow for a Freeze Warning. A Frost Advisory continues for north- \ncentral Wisconsin, with a 30 to 50 percent chance of temperatures \nfalling below 33 degrees across northern Forest and Florence \ncounties.\n\nOn Thursday and Friday nights, NBM percentiles also show a 30 to \n50 percent probability of low temperatures falling into the \nmid-30s across the Northwoods.\n\nStrong Wind Gusts On Tuesday:\nSoutherly return flow strengthens on Tuesday as high pressure\nslides east and a Pacific cold front approaches the Upper Midwest.\nWarm advection will push daytime maximum temperatures into the\nupper 60s to lower 70s across inland central and northern\nWisconsin. Tightening pressure gradients will produce gusty south-\nsouthwest winds, with NBM guidance showing a 60 to 80 percent\nprobability of wind gusts exceeding 30 mph Tuesday afternoon. \nMoisture transport remains limited, keeping precipitation \nprobabilities below 10 percent ahead of the frontal boundary.\n\nOutlook for Hazardous Marine Conditions Through Tuesday Night:\nBreezy northwest winds of 10 to 20 knots and waves of 2 to 4 feet\nacross northern Lake Michigan nearshore waters and Green Bay will\ncontinue to gradually subside by late afternoon. Surface high\npressure centered over the region will then maintain quiet marine\nconditions tonight through Monday, with winds under 15 knots \nbacking west to southwest Monday night and wave heights remaining \n2 feet or less.\n\nHazardous marine conditions are expected to return Tuesday into\nTuesday night as an advancing Pacific cold front tighten the \nregional pressure gradient. Southerly to south-southwesterly warm \nadvection winds will ramp up significantly Tuesday morning to 15 \nto 25 knots, with gusts reaching 25 to 30+ knots across nearshore \nzones and potential for marginal gales over open Lake Michigan \nwaters. These strong onshore winds will build waves to 5 to 9 \nfeet along the Lake Michigan shoreline and 3 to 5 feet on Green \nBay. Confidence is high that a Small Craft Advisory will be \nrequired for all Green Bay and Lake Michigan nearshore zones \nTuesday afternoon through Tuesday night before winds shift \nwesterly and subside behind the cold front Wednesday morning.",
